/* ==============================================================
   03 — Relative moves: approach and retract
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   MoveLinRelTrf, MoveLinRelWrf
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     A pick pattern that works at any pose, unchanged
   TIME      ~35 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE COMMANDS

       MoveLinRelTrf(x, y, z, alpha, beta, gamma)
       MoveLinRelWrf(x, y, z, alpha, beta, gamma)

   Same six arguments as MoveLin, but they are an OFFSET from
   where the robot already is, not an absolute destination. Both
   move in a straight line. The difference is which way the
   offset points:

     MoveLinRelTrf   relative to the TOOL frame — "back off 40 mm
                     along the tool axis", wherever the tool
                     happens to be pointing
     MoveLinRelWrf   relative to the WORLD frame — "straight up
                     40 mm", regardless of tool orientation

   WHY THIS IS MORE THAN A CONVENIENCE

   In basic/04 the approach pattern was written in absolute
   coordinates:

       MovePose(190, 0, 300, 0, 90, 0)     above the part
       MoveLin(190, 0, 260, 0, 90, 0)      down onto it
       MoveLin(190, 0, 300, 0, 90, 0)      back up

   Every one of those Z values has to be recomputed if the pick
   point moves. With a hundred pick points, that is a hundred
   pairs of numbers that can disagree with each other.

   Written relatively, the pattern becomes:

       MovePose(<the pick point>)
       MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 40, 0, 0, 0)    back off along tool
       ... approach, grip, retract ...

   Now there is ONE number per pick point, and the approach
   distance is stated once. Teach a new point and the approach
   comes along for free.

   TRF OR WRF?

   Use TRF when the motion is about the tool: withdraw along the
   axis of insertion, lift off the surface you are normal to,
   back a probe out of a hole. This is the usual answer, and it
   keeps working when the tool is at an angle.

   Use WRF when the motion is about the world: lift vertically to
   clear a fixture wall, step sideways along a pallet row. Use it
   when gravity or cell geometry defines the direction, not the
   tool.

   These are linear moves, so they obey SetCartLinVel and all the
   MoveLin caveats: they can be refused near singularities or
   joint limits, and they hold the arm configuration.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)
SetCartLinVel(60)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- The pick pattern, written once ----------------------------
   Below, the same five-line pattern runs at three different pick
   points in three different orientations. The pattern itself
   never changes — only the MovePose line that puts the robot at
   the point. */

// --- Pick point 1: tool pointing straight down ----------------
MovePose(190, -60, 260, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)     // approach along tool axis
Delay(0.5)                            // (grip here)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 40, 0, 0, 0)      // retract along tool axis

Delay(1)

// --- Pick point 2: same pattern, different place --------------
MovePose(190, 0, 280, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 40, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)

// --- Pick point 3: tilted 30 degrees --------------------------
// This is the one that proves the point. The tool is no longer
// vertical, and the approach lines are still unchanged — because
// "along the tool axis" follows the tool. In absolute
// coordinates this move would need trigonometry.
MovePose(190, 60, 260, 0, 60, 0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 40, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


/* --- TRF versus WRF, side by side -------------------------------
   Same tilted pose, same 40 mm, two different directions. */

MovePose(190, 0, 270, 0, 60, 0)
Delay(0.5)

// Along the tool axis — the tool backs straight out of where it
// was pointing, on the diagonal.
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 40, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)

// Straight up in world Z — vertical, regardless of the 30 degree
// tool tilt. Watch the two moves back to back and the difference
// is unmistakable.
MoveLinRelWrf(0, 0, 40, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelWrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)


// --- Home -----------------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Change every "40" in the pick patterns to "20" with a find
      and replace, and run again. The approach distance for the
      entire program just changed, in one edit. Compare that to
      editing absolute Z values for every point.

   2. The tilted pick is the strongest single demonstration here.
      Show it, then ask how their current system handles an
      approach vector that is not vertical.

   NEXT   04_posture_configuration.mxprog — the eight different
          ways to reach one pose.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
